--- machine\system\trust
canonical: Machine\System\Trust

What this machine trusts, and nothing else. The certificates themselves
that Mozilla ships are *not* here — they are package data at
`/usr/share/ca-certificates/mozilla.crt`, because `reg apply` is additive
and a root Mozilla withdrew would otherwise linger in the registry for
ever, while package upgrade already replaces payload correctly. What lives
here is what somebody decided: an addition, a distrust, a mode. That is
what keeps a domain audit legible.

trustd composes the shipped roots with this subtree and serves the result
on `/run/trustd/trust.sock`; the files under `/etc/ssl` are a rendering of
it, not the thing itself.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and Administrators
full, Authenticated Users read). That descriptor is the *only* gate on
changing what this machine trusts — `trust add` and `reg set` take the same
path, and there is deliberately no second permission model.

--- machine\system\trust generatelinuxtrustfiles
canonical: Machine\System\Trust GenerateLinuxTrustFiles
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
applies: live

Whether the compat files exist.

- `0` — no files. The machine's trust store is the socket alone. Software
  that reads a path finds nothing and fails with certificate errors; that
  is the honest outcome and the point of the setting. Suited to an
  appliance image whose whole software set speaks the socket. Switching to
  0 removes the files, because leaving stale trust in force is worse than
  having none.
- `1` — rendered and kept current (the default): `/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt`,
  `/etc/ssl/cert.pem` and the hashed `/etc/ssl/certs/` directory. Required
  while anything on the machine reads trust from a file, which today is
  nearly everything.
- `2` — reserved for serving the files through trustd itself per read.
  Treated as `1` until it exists.

--- machine\system\trust controlsecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Trust ControlSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: Everyone: TRUST_QUERY; SYSTEM, Administrators: all
applies: live

The trustd control object's descriptor, self-relative. Checked against the
peer's token on every request to `/run/trustd/trust.sock`. Rights:
`TRUST_QUERY` (0x1) to read the root set and the status, `TRUST_CONTROL`
(0x2) to force a recompose. Everyone may query by default because the root
set is public — in the default mode it is sitting in a world-readable file.

Note what this does not govern: adding or distrusting a certificate. Those
are registry writes under `Certificates\`, gated by that key's descriptor.

--- machine\system\trust\certificates\add
canonical: Machine\System\Trust\Certificates\Add

One subkey per certificate this machine trusts beyond the shipped set. The
subkey name is a label of the operator's choosing; it names the decision,
not the certificate.

Values on each subkey:

- `Certificate` (REG_BINARY): the certificate, DER. Written **last** when
  an entry is created — until it exists the entry is incomplete, and trustd
  skips incomplete entries rather than acting on half of one.
- `Purposes` (REG_MULTI_SZ, default `ServerAuth`): what the certificate is
  trusted for. v1 renders `ServerAuth` and carries the rest, so a
  code-signing or name-constrained root is a value change later rather than
  a schema change.

trustd validates every entry on read and never trusts the writer: an entry
that is not a certificate, is not a CA (`basicConstraints`), or has expired
is skipped with a warning, and the rest of the store is unaffected. One
operator's typo does not take the machine's trust down.

A package may *offer* an entry as a regim seed, inert until an image or an
administrator applies it. A package must never add to the system store by
installing: `peipkg install` widening what the whole machine trusts is the
supply-chain hole every distribution learned to close.

--- machine\system\trust\certificates\distrust
canonical: Machine\System\Trust\Certificates\Distrust

One value per certificate this machine must not trust, whatever it came
from. The value name is the certificate's SHA-256 fingerprint as 64 hex
digits — never a subject name, which is forgeable and reused. The data is a
REG_SZ reason, for whoever reads it later.

This is the emergency path, and the reason the shipped roots can be package
data: a distrust takes effect within seconds of the write, on one machine
or pushed domain-wide, with no package rebuild and no reboot. It applies to
shipped roots and to additions alike.

A distrust for a certificate the machine does not have is kept and reported
rather than discarded — if that root ever arrives, in a bundle upgrade or
an addition, it is already refused.
